  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1045 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nAfter a period of quiet, our next chance for active weather\narrives Wednesday as a mid-level shortwave and accompanying cold\nfront cross the region. Moisture and forcing along the front\nitself appear relatively meager, so continued the 20 to 30% \nchance across most of the area to signify some on and off\nscattered shower potential. Northerly flow then follows the cold \nfront passage by Wednesday night, so would expect some lake effect\nshowers to pick up overnight and last well through Thursday. This\nlake effect would primarily affect portions of far north-central\nWisconsin along the Upper Peninsula border and possible some areas\nalong the Lake Michigan shoreline including portions of Door,\nKewaunee, and Manitowoc as winds shift northeastwards Thursday.\nRain totals during the day Wednesday will be fairly light\neverywhere, with probabilistic guidance painting out only a 15-20%\nchance of exceeding a tenth of an inch. P-type will predominantly \nbe rain, except for a brief window for some light flurries late \nWednesday night and again Thursday night if lake effect showers in\nthe north sustain themselves.\n\nThe continued cold air advection through the latter part of the\nwork week will bring down high temperatures into the 40s for most\nof the area, which will be near or a degree or two below normal\nfor most of the area.",
